The user works in the React frontend. It provides screens for uploading datasets, preparing an analysis area, running analysis, and viewing results.
The frontend calls FastAPI. It sends project and dataset requests, uploads files, asks for validation, and starts analysis runs.
The backend stores project information and files locally. Project metadata is stored in JSON; scene bands, preparation data, and run outputs are kept in project folders.
The run API delegates analysis to a background worker. The current code uses an in-process ThreadPoolExecutor with two worker threads.
The GIS engine is the processing core. It validates input, applies the analysis mask, prepares the raster data, and calls the existing ONNX model.
The two modes take different paths.
Vegetation: one T1 scene is classified; no T1-versus-T2 change analysis is performed.
Wetland: T1 and T2 are aligned and classified; then GIS code calculates temporal changes and water-related evidence.
The engine writes outputs to disk. The API later reads those outputs to return result data, render map images, or serve a PDF report.